     "page": 1,
     "text": "RECEIVED 3  ##\nWilson PLC Medical Group\nRe: Aaron Harrison (DOB 10/05/1960)\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nI hope this letter finds you well. I am writing to provide a summary of Aaron Harrison’s medical history and current condition as pertains to his Type 1 diabetes. Mr. Harrison has been under my care since his diagnosis in December August 19, 2024, Mr. Harrison visited my office for a follow-up regarding his Type 1 diabetes. At this visit, his blood pressure was recorded at 125/88 mmHg, and his weight was 238 pounds. He is managing his condition well with his current insulin regimen, including insulin glargine 100 u/mL and insulin lispro 100 u/mL. His most recent A1c level was 9.4%, which reflects a slight increase from his previous measurement of 9.2%. Despite this, Mr. Harrison continues to adhere to his treatment plan, and his overall is stable.\n\nOn February 1, 2024, Mr. Harrison again came in for a diabetes management consultation. His blood pressure at that visit was 127/85 mmHg, and he weighed 236 pounds. He has been diagnosed with Type 1 diabetes, which was confirmed on December 3, 2022, when he was 61 years old. His A1c level at that time was 9.2%, a figure that has remained relatively consistent since his diagnosis. His diabetes is currently controlled with insulin therapy, including insulin glargine 100 u/mL and insulin lispro 100 u/mL. There are no signs of microalbuminuria, neuropathy, retinopathy, or coronary artery disease associated with his diabetes.\n\nDuring his visit on July 16, 2023, Mr. Harrison’s blood pressure was 132/82 mmHg, and he weighed 224 pounds. His diabetes management remained consistent with insulin therapy, and his A1c level was 9.2%. There were no complications and his condition continued to be stable.\n\nOn December 28, 2022, Mr. Harrison visited for his annual physical examination. His blood pressure was 124/91 mmHg, and he weighed 237 pounds. During this visit, he reported no new complaints and appeared to be doing well overall.\n\nOn June 10, 2022, Mr. Harrison was seen for a routine diabetes check-up. His blood pressure was 132/84 mmHg, and he weighed 236 pounds. A1c level was 9.2%, and he was doing well with his insulin therapy.\n\nOn November 22, 2021, Mr. Harrison visited for a diabetes follow-up. His blood pressure was 117/82 mmHg, and his weight was 237 pounds. His diabetes management was stable, and his A1c level remained at 9.2%.\n\nDuring his visit on May 6, 2021, Mr. Harrison’s blood pressure was 133/80 mmHg, and he weighed 226 pounds. His diabetes was being well managed insulin, and his A1c level was"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "9.2%.\n\nOn October 17, 2020, Mr. Harrison came in for a visit regarding a back strain. His blood pressure was 122/90 mmHg, and his weight was 227 pounds. During this visit, he was also assessed for his Type 1 diabetes, which had been diagnosed earlier in the year. His A1c level at that time was 9.2%, he was his condition with insulin therapy.\n\nOn March 31, 2020, Mr. Harrison visited for a routine diabetes check-up. His blood pressure was 128/85 mmHg, and he weighed 235 pounds. His A1c level was 9.2%, and his diabetes was being managed effectively with insulin.\n\nA summary of Mr. Harrison’s A1c levels over the past few years is as follows:\n- August 24, 2024: 9.4%\n- February 24, 2024: 9.2%\n- July 23, 2023: 9.2%\n- December 22, 2022: 9.2%\n\nIn terms of lifestyle factors, Mr. Harrison reports consuming approximately 8 alcoholic drinks per week. There is no documented history of tobacco use.\n\nPlease feel free to contact me if you require further information or regarding Mr. Harrison’s medical history and current condition.\n\nSincerely,\n\nDr. Danielle PLC Medical Group\nEndocrinology\n\n---\n\n[End of Letter]\n"
